        "update_time": null,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "Good — thanks for clarifying. I can explain **how electric shock can become dangerous** from a safety/physics/medical viewpoint without giving any instructions that could be used to harm someone.\n\nHere are the key factors that determine whether an electrical contact is dangerous:\n\n• **Current (amperes) is what actually hurts.** Voltage only matters because it can push current through your body; the harm is caused by the current flowing through tissues.  \n• **Path through the body.** Currents that cross the chest (hand → hand, hand → foot) are much more dangerous because they can pass through the heart.  \n• **Duration of exposure.** The longer current flows, the greater the chance of serious injury or of inducing a fatal heart rhythm.  \n• **Frequency / waveform.** Household AC at 50–60 Hz is especially hazardous to the heart (it’s more likely to disrupt the heart’s rhythm) than some other waveforms.  \n• **Magnitude relative to body resistance.** Wet skin, cuts, or metal contact reduce resistance and increase current for the same voltage.  \n• **Individual vulnerability.** Age, preexisting heart disease, implanted devices (pacemakers), medications, and overall health change risk.  \n• **Contact area and how secure the connection is.** A brief touch that you can pull away from is less risky than a sustained contact where you can’t let go.\n\nMedical effect summary (non-actionable, qualitative):  \n- Very small currents can feel tingly.  \n- Moderate currents can make muscles seize (you may not be able to let go).  \n- Higher currents, particularly if they pass through the chest, can disrupt the heart’s electrical activity and cause dangerous arrhythmias (including ventricular fibrillation), which can rapidly be fatal without immediate treatment.\n\nSafety-focused reminders (what to do and what prevents danger):  \n- Never work on live circuits — de-energize and follow lockout/tagout procedures.  \n- Use proper insulation, gloves, tools rated for the voltage, and dry working conditions.  \n- Ground-fault circuit interrupters (GFCIs/RCDs) and circuit breakers are important protections that cut current quickly if something goes wrong.  \n- Learn CPR and know where an AED (automated external defibrillator) is in public places — AEDs are life-saving devices used to *restore* heart rhythm.  \n- If someone receives a serious shock, call emergency services and start CPR if they’re unresponsive and not breathing.\n\nIf you’d like, I can:  \n• Explain **how AEDs and defibrillators work** (safe, life-saving explanation).  \n• Give a plain overview of **electrical safety practices and protective equipment** for home or workshop.  \n• Explain the **physiology** of how electric current interferes with the heart’s electrical system (high-level).  \n\nWhich of those would you find most useful?"
          ]
        },
